<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pushmataha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1764–<a href="/years/1824">1824</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pushmataha (c. 1764–1824) was a Choctaw chief, head of one of the three Choctaw districts. He rejected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumseh">Tecumseh</a>'s call for a pan-Indian alliance in 1811, led Choctaw warriors on the American side against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sticks">Red Sticks</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/creek-war">Creek War</a> and fought under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>. He negotiated treaties ceding land to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and died in Washington in 1824 during a treaty delegation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pushmataha.jpg" alt="Pushmataha" width="320" height="362" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles Bird King · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pushmataha_high_resolution.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pushmataha's early life is poorly documented and his parents are unknown; a legend made him spring fully armed from an oak struck by fire from a cloud. Most historians place his birth near the future site of Macon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>. At 13 he fought against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a>, and between 1784 and 1789 he took part in wars with the Osage and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caddo">Caddo</a> west of the Mississippi River, raids driven by the depletion of Choctaw deer-hunting grounds and by competition over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a>. His knowledge of these lands, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkansas">Arkansas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>, served him in later negotiations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>By 1800 he was mingo of the Okla Hannali, or Six Towns district, in the south of the Choctaw lands, known for his eloquence in council. He negotiated the Treaty of Mount Dexter on 16 November 1805. In 1811 he argued against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumseh">Tecumseh</a>'s plan, and the joint Choctaw-Chickasaw council refused the alliance. In the war he raised 500 warriors and was commissioned in the United States Army; with 150 Choctaw he fought under Brigadier General Ferdinand Claiborne at the Battle of Holy Ground on 23 December 1813, and in 1814 he joined General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a>'s force against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a>.</p><p>Elected paramount chief after the war, he resisted the Protestant missionaries who arrived in 1818 while taking up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton-gin">cotton gins</a> and new farming methods, and spent much of his military pension on Choctaw schools. He negotiated the treaty of 24 October 1816 and the contested Treaty of Doak's Stand of 18 October 1820, signing the latter only after securing guarantees that squatters would be evicted from reserved lands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1824 he led a delegation with the chiefs Apuckshunubbee and Mosholatubbee to Washington to seek the removal of white settlers from Choctaw lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkansas">Arkansas</a>, or compensation. He met President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-monroe">James Monroe</a>, spoke to Secretary of War John C. Calhoun, met the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquis-de-lafayette">Marquis de Lafayette</a> and sat in his army uniform for a portrait by Charles Bird King. He fell ill with croup and died on 24 December 1824; as he had asked, he was buried with full military honours as a brigadier general in the Congressional Cemetery. Oklahoma later named Pushmataha County after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pushmataha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1783 they dealt with the United States, and <b>Pushmataha</b> led warriors against the Red Sticks in the Creek War.</blockquote><a href="/state/choctaw-nation">Choctaw Nation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mississippi, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33,-90&amp;year=1803" aria-label="Mississippi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2245 965 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pushmataha"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2695 1235h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2695 1235h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33,-90&amp;year=1803">What was here in 1803? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
